## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates practical knowledge of securing software supply chains in cloud-native environments, addressing real-world security risks that SaaS platforms face when handling third-party plugins and dependencies.

## Key Concepts

- SBOM (Software Bill of Materials) generation and analysis
- Dependency pinning with cryptographic hashes
- Plugin attestation and private registry management
- Pull request gates for drift detection and reproducible builds
- Incident response protocols for compromised plugins (72-hour SLA)

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: Attestation verification for plugin registry
const verifyPluginAttestation = async (pluginId, attestation) => {
  const isValidSignature = await crypto.verify(attestation.signature, publicKey);
  if (!isValidSignature) throw new Error('Invalid plugin attestation');

  const sbom = await generateSBOM(pluginId);
  const hasVulnerabilities = await scanVulnerabilities(sbom);

  return { isValidSignature, hasVulnerabilities, sbom };
};
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests ability to design practical, compliant data access controls across serverless and containerized services, with ABAC, key management, RBAC, policy-as-code, and IR planning.

## Key Concepts
- ABAC attributes and enforcement points
- MongoDB Atlas RBAC & Client-Side Encryption
- AWS KMS key management and rotation
- Policy-as-code (OPA) integration and CI gates
- Incident response timing and evidence collection

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-authorization check example
function hasAccess(attrs, request) {
  const {service, tenant, data_class, operation, region} = attrs;
  const r = request;
  return r.service === service && r.tenant === tenant && r.data_class === data_class && r.operation === operation && r.region === region;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test ABAC policies in CI/CD and simulate breaches?
- How would you audit data access for forensics and regulatory reporting?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests practical risk reduction for credential leakage in automated data exports, ensuring tenants are isolated and tokens are short-lived with clear containment procedures.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant least privilege with STS AssumeRole
- Short-lived credentials and rotation
- Secrets management and tenant-scoped IAM policies
- CloudTrail/GuardDuty for anomaly detection
- IR playbook with containment and recovery steps

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: AssumeRole for a tenant export job using AWS SDK v3
const { STSClient, AssumeRoleCommand } = require("@aws-sdk/client-sts");
const sts = new STSClient({ region: "us-east-1" });
const cmd = new AssumeRoleCommand({
  RoleArn: tenantRoleArn,
  RoleSessionName: "export-tenant-"+tenantId,
  DurationSeconds: 900
});
const resp = await sts.send(cmd);
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test the mitigation against token replay?
- What metrics would indicate token misuse?

